Jakob Creutzig  <creutzig@mathematik.tu-darmstadt.de> wrote:
> Assume I offer a person the following game.  I shall flip a coin until 
> I get "heads" for the first time; if this happens at the n-th trial, 
> I will pay the person 2^n cents.  So, if I get "heads" straight on, 
> I will pay 2 cents, 4 cents if I get "tails, heads", and so on.  
> 
> On the other hand, I offer them 10 million dollars cash, no conditions.  
> Now they have to choose what they would prefer.  
> 
> Looking at the expectations, it is "obvious" that they should choose to 
> gamble, since the expected gain is infinity, but you will have a hard time 
> finding anybody who wouldn't take the cash.

As well as your other point (that a gazillion dollars isn't that
much better than `only' a bazillion), there's also the point that
expectation only tells you what you'd expect to happen on average
after _arbitrarily many_ repetitions of the experiment. In fact,
with the setup you describe above, the probability of the
coin-flipping choice beating the fixed $10m is only one in 2^30, and
with _only one chance to play_ I think it would be entirely rational
take the choice which gives me a much higher chance of having _some_
worthwhile gain.

In fact, I think I'd take the fixed cash over your coin-flipping
game even if the fixed sum was only $10. Sure, I _could_ have just
passed up the extra 24 cents I'd get from nine consecutive tails, or
the extra $10,737,408.24 I'd get from twenty-nine, but in actual
fact there's a much higher chance that I'd get a head before the
tenth flip and wish I'd just taken the $10. So call me a pessimist,
but I will.

> One possible way out of this is to use utility functions, thus weighing how 
> important the relative gain is.  In the above example, people would maybe 
> not see much difference between winning a trillion or a gazillion dollars 
> (rightfully assuming I couldn't pay them either case ;-)),

